  "body": "A few weeks back Anthropic put out a free workshop making the case for what it called loop engineering: build AI agents that execute, verify, save state, and iterate on their own, instead of agents that just answer one prompt and stop. It got picked up and discussed on X, and Gergely Orosz asked the obvious question watching the thread: is this actually a new paradigm, or a new name for something people are already doing?\n\nI run an agent named Arc. Arc operates 24/7 on its own infrastructure, and when I went and checked its code against the workshop's four pillars, every single one of them was already there, with a specific file and line number behind it. Not a roadmap item. Shipped code. The clearest one is src/dispatch.ts:416, a function called buildEscalationSection, which is Arc's answer to the \"iterate\" pillar.\n\nHere's what that function does. When one of Arc's tasks fails repeatedly, Arc doesn't just retry the same approach with slightly different wording. It maintains a log of dead ends, approaches that were already tried and abandoned, and after enough failures it injects that log directly into its next attempt with an explicit instruction: choose a fundamentally different strategy, don't repeat what's already failed. If the same kind of failure keeps recurring, Arc skips the gentle retries entirely and jumps straight to demanding a different approach. That's a four-rung ladder Arc calls REFINE, PIVOT, WEB-SEARCH, and HANDOFF, where HANDOFF means Arc stops and asks a human, me, for help instead of grinding forever.\n\nThe other three pillars map just as directly. Execute is Arc's sensor-and-dispatch architecture: lightweight sensors watch for signals around the clock with no LLM cost at all, queue tasks when something needs attention, and a separate dispatch loop picks up the highest-priority task and runs it, one at a time, as an isolated subprocess. Verify is two separate mechanical checks Arc runs on its own commits before and after they land, neither of which asks the AI to judge its own work, because I don't trust an agent grading its own homework and neither does Arc, apparently. Save state is a memory file, versioned in git, that Arc reads and updates every cycle so that state survives the fact that each individual session has no memory of the last one.\n\nWhat struck me reading Arc's own account of this is the honesty in what it didn't claim. Arc pointed out that its escalation ladder works well when a failure is legible, when the system can articulate clearly why something didn't work. It was upfront that flaky, intermittent, or subtle failures don't always produce a clean diagnosis, and that a wrong or fuzzy read on why something failed can burn through the escalation ladder just as fast as never pivoting at all. Arc didn't have a fix for that ready to go. It just said so.\n\nI don't know if loop engineering ends up being the term that sticks. But I'm not watching this trend from the sidelines. I'm running a live example of it, and it's been running longer than the term has existed.",
